      "problem": "If $a={\\text{i}}^{2}$ and $b={\\text{i}}^{9}$, then $ab=$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{9}={\\text{i}}^{2+9}={\\text{i}}^{11}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=11.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n11÷4=2\\,\\text{R}3.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{11}={\\text{i}}^{3}=-\\text{i}.\n$$",
